      <section class="content-block">
        <h2>8th Central Pay Commission: Strategic Framework & Key Projections</h2>
        <p>
          The constitution and implementation of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong> represents an essential financial revision for more than 48 lakh Central Government employees and over 100 lakh state government staff across India. Anticipated implementation between 2026–2027, the 8th CPC will follow the landmark 7th Pay Commission recommendations introduced in 2016 and set new benchmarks for public sector compensation, pension structures, and cost-of-living indexation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Historically, the Government of India institutes a Central Pay Commission approximately every ten years. The 6th Pay Commission was notified in 2006, followed by the landmark 7th Pay Commission in 2015. Given that decade-long intervals have become standard administrative practice, the constitution of the 8th Pay Commission is now anticipated with high urgency by government employee unions, financial planners, and administrative bodies seeking clarity on revised salary structures, fitment factors, and long-term pension liabilities.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-block">
        <h2>Fitment Factor Analysis: How Basic Pay Revisions Are Formulated</h2>
        <p>
          The primary mathematical driver of basic pay and pension revisions in Indian Pay Commissions is the <strong>Fitment Factor</strong>. This uniform multiplier converts existing 7th CPC Basic Pay into 8th CPC Basic Pay across all levels and grades. A fitment factor of 2.28, for example, means that a current basic pay of ₹35,400 becomes ₹80,712 (35400 × 2.28).
        </p>
        <p>
          For the 8th Pay Commission, the Dearness Allowance crossed the 50% milestone and continues its upward trajectory under AICPI-IW datasets. Consequently, fitment factor scenarios for the 8th CPC are anticipated to range between 1.92x (conservative baseline accounting for DA neutrality) and 2.86x (maximum staff-side demand for consumption stimulus).
        </p>
        
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Fitment Scenario</th>
                <th>Proposed Factor</th>
                <th>Min Pay (Level 1)</th>
                <th>Level 6 (GP 4200)</th>
                <th>Overall Net Hike Impact</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Conservative (DA Neutral Floor)</strong></td>
                <td>1.92x</td>
                <td>₹34,560</td>
                <td>₹67,968</td>
                <td>Protects against inflation; modest real wage growth</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Working Baseline (NC-JCM Proposal)</strong></td>
                <td>2.28x</td>
                <td>₹41,040</td>
                <td>₹80,712</td>
                <td>Balanced hike mirroring industrial compensation trends</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Parity Model (7th CPC Equivalent)</strong></td>
                <td>2.57x</td>
                <td>₹46,260</td>
                <td>₹90,978</td>
                <td>Strong consumption stimulus for salaried central employees</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Staff Side Maximum Demand</strong></td>
                <td>2.86x</td>
                <td>₹51,480</td>
                <td>₹1,01,244</td>
                <td>Aggressive compensation adjustment countering urban cost surges</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Under the widely supported <strong>2.28 Fitment Factor</strong>, an employee currently serving at Pay Level 1 (with a minimum basic pay of ₹18,000) will see their base pay climb to approximately ₹41,040. This translates to a real purchasing power improvement of ~33% after accounting for inflation trends observed over the 2016–2026 decade.
        </p>
      </section>

